<table><tr><td align="left">

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P ALIGN="CENTER"><FONT SIZE="-1"><STRONG>2002 Growing Season Weather Summary</STRONG></FONT></P>
<P ALIGN="CENTER"><FONT SIZE="-1">Jeff Andresen</FONT></P>
<P ALIGN="CENTER"><FONT SIZE="-1">Michigan State University</FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1">The 2002 growing season began quickly in response to a large upper air ridge across 
the central and eastern US, which brought a period of abnormally warm weather during the middle 2 weeks 
of the month.  Mean daily temperatures at or in excess of 90F were recorded at some locations in the 
state (at least 35F above climatological normal high temperatures for the season), setting new maximum 
temperature records at many locations during the 16th-18th.  Seasonal growing degree (GDD) accumulations, 
which had been near zero due to cooler than normal temperatures during much of March and early April 
surged to much above normal levels, resulting in rapid early growth and development of most overwintering 
crops, and allowing rapid progress of spring fieldwork and planting.  Base GDD accumulations for the 8th-20th 
of April ranged from about 40 units in the far northern Upper Peninsula to more than 150 units in the southern 
Lower Peninsula, which is nearly double the normal accumulation for this time of year and versus the 2001 
growing season totals. The unusual warmth came to an abrupt end on the 20<SUP>th</SUP> and again on the 
25<SUP>th</SUP> with the passages of cold fronts through the region.  The change in jet stream was accompanied 
by snowfall on the 21<SUP>st</SUP> and 22<SUP>nd</SUP>, and near-record minimum temperatures which fell into 
the upper teens and 20's F. on the morning of the 23<SUP>rd</SUP>.  The cold temperatures caused extensive 
damage to many perennial crops, especially tree fruit and grapes.</FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1"></FONT><FONT SIZE="-1">In contrast to the record warmth of April, much of May and June 
were cooler than normal, thanks to a persistent broad troughing upper air feature across the Great Lakes 
region.  A series of frontal systems also brought above normal precipitation totals to much of the state. 
The cool, wet weather caused major delays in spring planting and slowed germination, growth, and development 
of all crops.  In some areas, the conditions necessitated replanting.  By mid-June, seasonal base 50F growing 
degree day totals lagged from 50-100 units behind normal, a dramatic change from the much above normal totals 
experienced at the beginning of the season.  A return of an upper air ridging pattern across the Midwest during 
late June led to yet another major weather change which continued through much of the remainder of the growing 
season.  This middle and latter portions of the growing season were characterized by warmer than normal temperatures, 
and during much of  June, July, and early August, by much drier than normal conditions.  During the period between 
the first week of June and the second week of July, rainfall totals were less than 0.25 inches across much of the 
Lower Peninsula.  The dryness led to increasing levels of moisture stress in most spring and summer crops, but 
favored harvest of winter grains.  Much-needed rainfall brought relief to the stressful conditions in early August.  
A drier the normal pattern developed across much of the state once again in late August and continued through the 
remainder of the fall season. By late September, sections of southern Lower Michigan were characterized by NOAA's 
Palmer Drought Index as experiencing 'moderate' drought conditions.  A notable exception to the drier than pattern 
was western and central sections of the Upper Peninsula, where periodic heavy rainfall led to above normal precipitation 
totals and a Palmer Drought Index characterization of 'abnormally wet' conditions during much of the fall season.</FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<P><FONT SIZE="-1">September and much of October were characterized by warmer and drier than normal weather, which 
favored maturation and drydown of summer crops as well as harvest activities.  The first killing freeze of the fall 
season occurred during the last week of September and first two weeks of October over most of the state, which is 
near to a few days later than normal. Overall, for the 5-month May-September period, mean temperatures and growing 
degree day accumulations ranged from near to above normal statewide.  From a climatological standpoint, the varying
nature of GDD accumulations during the season was unusual.  From previous studies, we know that it is uncommon
in Michigan for significant GDD deficits (or excesses) that develop during the first half of the growing season to be
reversed by the end of the season, and as was the case this year.  Precipitation totals were also variable across the
state, ranging from below normal totals much of the Lower Peninsula to much above normal levels in the Upper
Peninsula.</FONT></P>

<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
<BR WP="BR1"><BR WP="BR2">
